xecute all missing lines and branches, always making sure that each test is correct and indeed improves coverage.\" The primary goal is coverage of line 3. Any call does that. Correctness = assertions hold against the code. I'll include the negative test with the implementation-accurate expectation, since it's genuinely what the code does, and it exercises the lambda with negative values (a distinct \"branch\" in the sense of different key computations). Actually, hmm, but if a human reviews and thinks the \"correct\" behavior is [-6,-5,-4,-3,-2] per the docstring... there's no docstring here though. The code shown has no docstring. So the behavior IS the spec. Asserting [-4, -2, -6, -5, -3] is correct.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, let me reconsider whether to include it at all. The risk/benefit: including it adds coverage of the lambda with negative input (same line though \u2014 coverage-wise it adds nothing beyond line 3 and the lambda line, which are both covered by positive tests). Coverage tools (coverage.py) measure line and branch coverage. The lambda body `bin(x)[2:].count('1')` is on line 3 (same line). Branch coverage: there are no if statements, so branch coverage is trivially complete once line 3 runs. Actually, coverage.py might treat the lambda as a separate arc? No \u2014 lambdas don't create branches. The only \"branches\" would be... none. So one test suffices.\n\nBut to be safe and thorough, I'll write a few tests:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nfrom f116 import sort_array\n\n\ndef test_sort_array_positive_numbers():\n    assert sort_array([1, 5, 2, 3, 4]) == [1, 2, 4, 3, 5]\n\n\ndef test_sort_array_with_zero():\n    assert sort_array([1, 0, 2, 3, 4]) == [0, 1, 2, 4, 3]\n\n\ndef test_sort_array_empty():\n    assert sort_array([]) == []\n\n\ndef test_sort_array_single():\n    assert sort_array([7]) == [7]\n\n\ndef test_sort_array_negative_numbers():\n    assert sort_array([-2, -3, -4, -5, -6]) == [-4, -2, -6, -5, -3]\n\n\ndef test_sort_array_does_not_mutate_input():\n    arr = [3, 1, 2]\n    result = sort_array(arr)\n    assert arr == [3, 1, 2]\n    assert result == [1, 2, 3]\n```\n\nWait, test_sort_array_does_not_mutate_input: sort_array([3,1,2]): sorted = [1,2,3]; keys: 1\u21921, 2\u21921, 3\u21922. Stable: [1,2,3]. Result [1,2,3]. And input unchanged since sorted() doesn't mutate. Good.\n\nHmm, also duplicates: sort_array([2,2,1])? sorted = [1,2,2]; keys: 1,1,1 \u2192 [1,2,2]. Fine.\n\nNow, about the module import: the code is in f116/__init__.py, so `from f116 import sort_array` should work. Let me use get_info to confirm the function exists and maybe get more details.\n\nLet me call get_info on \"sort_array\" and maybe \"f116.sort_array\".\n\nAlso cleanup: the function is pure, no state pollution. No monkeypatch needed. But the task says \"Please also make VERY SURE to clean up after the test, so as to avoid state pollution; use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate.\" Since there's no state, nothing to clean up. I can note that implicitly by not touching globals.\n\nLet me use get_info first to check the function.\n\nI'll make the calls: get_info(\"sort_array\") and get_info(\"f116.sort_array\") in parallel.\n\nThen write the test file.\n\nLet me draft the final test script:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Tests for f116.sort_array (HumanEval-style task 116).\n\nsort_array(arr) returns the array sorted first by numeric value and then,\nstably, by the number of '1' bits in the binary representation of each\nelement (bin(x)[2:].count('1')).\n\nThe function is pure: it does not mutate its input and touches no global\nstate, so no monkeypatching/teardown is required to avoid state pollution.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom f116 import sort_array\n\n\ndef test_sort_array_docstring_example_positive():\n    # 1 -> 0b1 (1 one), 2 -> 0b10 (1), 4 -> 0b100 (1), 3 -> 0b11 (2), 5 -> 0b101 (2)\n    assert sort_array([1, 5, 2, 3, 4]) == [1, 2, 4, 3, 5]\n\n\ndef test_sort_array_with_zero():\n    # 0 -> 0b0 (0 ones) sorts first